    "tie_set": [],
-   "outcome": "Tile-size characterisation across 256/384/512 px (F1@20m), gemini-3-flash, GS 4-map curator GT. The optimal tile size is ARCHITECTURE-DEPENDENT, consistent with the FP-filtering mechanism (Obs 351, Obs 179, Obs 171). CLEAN ISOLATION (hold architecture x modality x thinking x temperature constant, vary only size): single-pass text climbs monotonically with tile size (256=0.342 < 384=0.520 < 512=0.606) -- with no FP filter, larger tiles give cleaner proposals; single-pass image is flat (384~0.600 ~ 512~0.586). Text MINIMAL consensus still prefers 512 (+0.02..+0.05 over 384 across T0.3/0.7/1.0) -- a weak filter. Text HIGH consensus FLIPS to 384 (T0.7 0.814 > 512 0.773; T0.3 0.789 > 0.774; T1.0 ~tie) -- the strong diversity+vote filter realises 384's higher recall. CONSENSUS+VERIFIER head-to-head (View 3; Stage-D carry-forward adversarial verifier, gemini-3-flash minimal T=0.0 n=1; 14-buf+MCC; best prob_t per cell): 384=0.890 > 256=0.856 > 512=0.793 -- best=384px (delta +0.034 over 256). Two findings: (1) 384 STILL leads at consensus+PV, as it does at consensus-only, confirming the Obs 179 prior (NOT a surprise); (2) the verifier RESCUES 256 (Obs 352): 256 consensus jumps from 0.460 consensus-only (WORST) to 0.856 consensus+PV (+0.396), LEAPFROGGING 512 to take second -- so the per-architecture order is single-pass 512>384>256, consensus-only 384>512>256, consensus+PV 384>256>512. Obs 351's open question -- does 256 overwhelm even the verifier? -- is now answered: NO, the verifier rescues 256, but 384 retains the lead. CAVEAT: the 256 PV cell is the plain text 5-of-5 (N=5) family, whereas 384/512 share the HIGH-text N=30 lineage (HIGH-text not run at 256), so 256 stays a thin-provenance anchor; tile size is confounded with tile set (1032/487/340), so F1@20m is the cross-size headline and tile-MCC is reported per size, never differenced. BEST-ACHIEVABLE FLASH CEILING per size (consensus-only): 384 (0.814) > 512 (0.775) > 256 (0.460); single-pass 512 (0.631) > 384 (0.520) > 256 (0.342). Pro single-pass ran only at 384 (pro-text 0.792, pro-image MCC 0.868) -- context, not a tile-size comparison. Artefacts: results/tile-size-sweep/tile_size_sweep.{json,md} (now incl. View 3); recon results/era1-pv-stage-d/384-leg-recon.md; script scripts/tile_size_sweep.py.",
+   "outcome": "Tile-size characterisation across 256/384/512 px (F1@20m), gemini-3-flash, GS 4-map curator GT. The optimal tile size is ARCHITECTURE-DEPENDENT, consistent with the FP-filtering mechanism (Obs 351, Obs 179, Obs 171). CLEAN ISOLATION (hold architecture x modality x thinking x temperature constant, vary only size): single-pass text climbs monotonically with tile size (256=0.342 < 384=0.520 < 512=0.606) -- with no FP filter, larger tiles give cleaner proposals; single-pass image is flat (384~0.600 ~ 512~0.586). Text MINIMAL consensus still prefers 512 (+0.02..+0.05 over 384 across T0.3/0.7/1.0) -- a weak filter. Text HIGH consensus FLIPS to 384 (T0.7 0.814 > 512 0.773; T0.3 0.789 > 0.774; T1.0 ~tie) -- the strong diversity+vote filter realises 384's higher recall. CONSENSUS+VERIFIER head-to-head (View 3; Stage-D carry-forward adversarial verifier, gemini-3-flash minimal T=0.0 n=1; 14-buf+MCC; best prob_t per cell): 384=0.890 > 256=0.856 > 512=0.793 -- best=384px (delta +0.034 over 256). Two findings: (1) 384 STILL leads at consensus+PV, as it does at consensus-only, confirming the Obs 179 prior (NOT a surprise); (2) the verifier RESCUES 256 (Obs 352): 256 consensus jumps from 0.460 consensus-only (WORST) to 0.856 consensus+PV (+0.396), LEAPFROGGING 512 to take second -- so the per-architecture order is single-pass 512>384>256, consensus-only 384>512>256, consensus+PV 384>256>512. Obs 351's open question -- does 256 overwhelm even the verifier? -- is now answered: NO, the verifier rescues 256, but 384 retains the lead. CAVEAT: the 256 PV cell is the plain text 5-of-5 (N=5) family, whereas 384/512 share the HIGH-text N=30 lineage (HIGH-text not run at 256), so 256 stays a thin-provenance anchor; tile size is confounded with tile set (1032/487/340), so F1@20m is the cross-size headline and tile-MCC is reported per size, never differenced. BEST-ACHIEVABLE FLASH CEILING per size (consensus-only): 384 (0.814) > 512 (0.775) > 256 (0.460); single-pass 512 (0.631) > 384 (0.520) > 256 (0.342). Pro single-pass ran only at 384 (pro-text 0.792, pro-image MCC 0.868) -- context, not a tile-size comparison. Artefacts: results/tile-size-sweep/tile_size_sweep.{json,md} (now incl. View 3); recon results/era1-pv-stage-d/384-leg-recon.md; script scripts/tile_size_sweep.py. [AMENDED 2026-09-14, E88] View 2's best_per_size.by_arch_modality grid grouped cells by a modality assigned from a substring of the condition label, and retest-phase2e::canonical-last -- an exemplar-ORDERING variant whose pool carries neither modality token -- fell through to the TEXT leg although it transmitted an image-bearing configuration. Two of the fifteen legs swap leaders, both at 512 px single-pass: single-pass/image becomes retest-phase2e::canonical-last F1 0.6314 / MCC 0.2132 (was retest-phase2d::image-terse 0.6052 / 0.2239), and single-pass/text becomes retest-phase2c::text-scale-4 F1 0.6094 / MCC undefined (was canonical-last 0.6314 / 0.2132). The other thirteen legs are unchanged. Consequently the 'BEST-ACHIEVABLE FLASH CEILING per size ... single-pass 512 (0.631) > 384 (0.520) > 256 (0.342)' ladder above -- whose 384 and 256 entries are the single-pass/TEXT leg while its 512 entry was canonical-last -- reads 0.609 > 0.520 > 0.342 as the text leg and 0.631 > 0.600 > 0.342 as the overall per-size ceiling; the ordering 512 > 384 > 256 and the analysis's conclusion (the optimal tile size is architecture-dependent) hold under both readings. Views 1 and 3 -- the clean tile-size isolation and the consensus+verifier head-to-head, which hold modality FIXED rather than grouping by it -- are byte-identical, so 256=0.342 < 384=0.520 < 512=0.606, the HIGH-consensus flip to 384, View 3's 384=0.890 > 256=0.856 > 512=0.793, Obs 351 and Obs 352 all stand. The artefacts are regenerated at this commit and the 2026-06-09-vintage pair is archived at archive/superseded-sweeps/tile-size-sweep-pre-e88-2026-09-14/ with a delta table; beyond the E88 swap the regeneration also carries register growth (a 384 proposer-verifier/image leg, verified-adv-image-min-6of10 0.789 / 0.8032, and two Pro proposer-verifier rows in the Pro note -- 'Pro single-pass ran only at 384' is still true) and inherits E81's undefined-MCC convention (two 512 px View-1 text cells' mcc 0.0 -> null) plus one MCC re-read (384 px image T0.7, 0.3295 -> 0.3296); none of those four is an E88 effect and none changes a figure quoted above. Recomputation: results/modality-track-audit-2026-09-14/recomputation.md section 2; deltas reports/modality-rulings-deltas-2026-09-14.md.",
